#!/usr/bin/env ssc
---
name: js-derives-segmented
description: a `derives` instance must exist on the SEGMENTED codegen path too — the one `emit-js` (and the conformance JS lane) actually uses
backends: [int, js, jvm]
---

# `derives` must survive the SEGMENTED JS codegen path

`BUGS.md` `js-lane-missing-derives-and-coroutinecancel`. `JsGen.genModule` called
`emitMirrorAndDerives`; `JsGen.genModuleSegmented` did not. `ssc run-js` goes through the first
and worked; `ssc emit-js` goes through the second — and so does the conformance JS lane, which
runs `ssc-tools emit-js <case> | node`. Every module compiled that way got **no Mirror and no
derived instance at all**, so `summon[TC[T]]` emitted a bare `TC_T` and the bundle died with
`ReferenceError: TC_T is not defined`. Not the tree shaker: `--no-tree-shake` failed identically.

Both scan paths are pinned, because only the second was ever suspected and they are different
code:

1. **same-module** typeclass — `object Tagged` with a `def derived` declared right here;
2. **imported** typeclass — `Labelled`, from `./lib/derives-schema.ssc`, which reaches
   `emitMirrorAndDerives` only via `importedTypeclassObjects`.

The `elemLabels.length` in each derived value is what proves the *Mirror* was emitted too, not
just some object under the right name: a Mirror that never got its labels would report `0`.

The import sits ABOVE the fence on purpose: an import written *inside* a fence is a separate,
still-open divergence (`BUGS.md` `v2-native-front-in-fence-imports-not-followed`), and this case
must fail for its own reason or not at all.

[Labelled, LabelledInstance](./lib/derives-schema.ssc)

```scalascript
trait Tagged[A]:
  def tag(): String

case class TaggedInstance(text: String) extends Tagged[Any]:
  def tag(): String = text

object Tagged:
  def derived(m: Mirror): Tagged[Any] =
    TaggedInstance(m.label + "#" + m.elemLabels.length.toString)

case class Point(x: Int, y: Int) derives Tagged

case class Person(name: String, age: Int, city: String) derives Labelled

println(summon[Tagged[Point]].tag())
println(summon[Labelled[Person]].label())
```
